   {
    "id": "8f41e85c-d571-4e26-9be8-aebe07fd2966",
    "title": "Longformer: The Long-Document Transformer",
    "kind": "literature",
    "path": ".research/sources/8f41e85c-d571-4e26-9be8-aebe07fd2966/reference.json",
    "sha256": "774ffa8ad1b7b497378d3165909452010232799f28f68a2b505ab5857d75d34b",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:23:54.255Z",
    "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2004.05150",
    "doi": "10.48550/arxiv.2004.05150",
    "fullTextPath": ".research/sources/8f41e85c-d571-4e26-9be8-aebe07fd2966/fulltext.pdf",
    "coverage": "full-text",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "locator": {
       "key": "abstract"
      },
      "text": "The quadratic complexity of standard attention (O(N²)) remains the dominant bottleneck for training and deploying large language models on long sequences. We introduce Murmurative Attention, a novel attention mechanism that replaces pairwise token-token interactions with token-to-slot interactions over a fixed-size learnable memory pool of M slots"
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 1
      },
      "text": "To address this limitation,\nwe introduce the Longformer with an attention\nmechanism that scales linearly with sequence\nlength, making it easy to process documents of\nthousands of tokens or longer.\nLongformer’s\nattention mechanism is a drop-in replacement\nfor the standard self-attention and combines\na local windowed attention with a task moti-\nvated global attention.\nFollowing prior work\non long-sequence transformers, we evaluate\nLongformer on character-level language mod-\neling and achieve state-of-the-art results on\ntext8 and enwik8.\nOur pretrained Longformer consistently out-\nperforms RoBERTa on long document tasks\nand sets new state-of-the-art results on Wiki-\nHop and TriviaQA.\nWe ﬁnally introduce the\nLongformer-Encoder-Decoder (LED), a Long-\nformer variant for supporting long document\ngenerative sequence-to-sequence tasks, and\ndemonstrate its effectiveness on the arXiv sum-\nmarization dataset."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 2
      },
      "text": "After pretraining, we apply it to\ndownstream language tasks through ﬁnetuning and\ndemonstrate that Longformer consistently outper-\nforms RoBERTa on a wide range of document-level\nnatural language tasks including text classiﬁcation,\nQA, and coreference resolution, achieving state-of-\nthe-art results on two of these datasets."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 3
      },
      "text": "BigBird (Zaheer et al., 2020) is an exten-\nsion over ETC with evaluation on additional tasks,\nincluding summarization."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "58177e3f-13c1-4855-9f52-653d2ba56393",
    "title": "Big Bird: Transformers for Longer Sequences",
    "kind": "literature",
    "path": ".research/sources/58177e3f-13c1-4855-9f52-653d2ba56393/reference.json",
    "sha256": "f6d867b6120d59359379ffe4f734bbde843c8fc7485581d8263e27908f2d5142",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:23:57.808Z",
    "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2007.14062",
    "doi": "10.48550/arxiv.2007.14062",
    "fullTextPath": ".research/sources/58177e3f-13c1-4855-9f52-653d2ba56393/fulltext.pdf",
    "coverage": "full-text",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "locator": {
       "key": "abstract"
      },
      "text": "To remedy this, we propose, BigBird, a sparse attention mechanism that reduces this quadratic dependency to linear."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 1
      },
      "text": "To remedy this, we propose, BIGBIRD , a sparse\nattention mechanism that reduces this quadratic dependency to linear.\nAs\na consequence of the capability to handle longer context, BIGBIRD drastically\nimproves performance on various NLP tasks such as question answering and\nsummarization."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 7
      },
      "text": "both BIGBIRD and Longformer perform better than limited length RoBERTa, withBIGBIRD -ETC\nperforming the best."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 8
      },
      "text": "One can clearly see the importance of using longer context as both Longformer and BIGBIRD\noutperform models with smaller contexts."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "bde31200-fb67-4bea-9cf0-ccbeaae4bacc",
    "title": "RULER: What's the Real Context Size of Your Long-Context Language Models?",
    "kind": "literature",
    "path": ".research/sources/bde31200-fb67-4bea-9cf0-ccbeaae4bacc/reference.json",
    "sha256": "4d67cf9cc3ef0615d1be0da544b8d9ff795505e8c8a8b069508b963b8efb1daf",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:24:00.987Z",
    "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2404.06654",
    "doi": "10.48550/arxiv.2404.06654",
    "fullTextPath": ".research/sources/bde31200-fb67-4bea-9cf0-ccbeaae4bacc/fulltext.pdf",
    "coverage": "full-text",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 1
      },
      "text": "The needle-in-a-haystack (NIAH) test, which examines the ability to re-\ntrieve a piece of information (the “needle”) from long distractor texts (the\n“haystack”), has been widely adopted to evaluate long-context language\nmodels (LMs).\nTo provide a more\ncomprehensive evaluation of long-context LMs, we create a new synthetic\nbenchmark RULER with flexible configurations for customized sequence\nlength and task complexity."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 6
      },
      "text": "For each\ntask, we evaluate each model with 500 examples generated for each length from the series\n(4K, 8K, 16K, 32K, 64K, 128K), while complying with each model’s necessary chat template."
     }
    ]
   },
