    "id": "dfdeb724-ff72-451b-bb34-2e221107db1b",
    "title": "FlashAttention-3: Fast and Accurate Attention with Asynchrony and Low-precision",
    "kind": "literature",
    "path": ".research/sources/dfdeb724-ff72-451b-bb34-2e221107db1b/reference.json",
    "sha256": "d9c25a05f1c11f7c47d861a5d862313d81e4292f7c562cce46ebd0ba714df9eb",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:24:05.227Z",
    "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2407.08608",
    "doi": "10.48550/arxiv.2407.08608",
    "fullTextPath": ".research/sources/dfdeb724-ff72-451b-bb34-2e221107db1b/fulltext.pdf",
    "coverage": "full-text",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 2
      },
      "text": "To this end, we proposeFlashAttention-3, which contributes and synthesizes three new ideas to further\nimprove performance on newer GPU architectures\nWe also validate that FP16FlashAttention-3 yields the same\nnumerical error asFlashAttention-2 and is better than the standard attention implementation as intermediate\nresults (e.g., softmax rescaling) are kept in FP32."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 12
      },
      "text": "We are\nable to speed up attention by 1.5-2.0×times compared toFlashAttention-2, and reduce FP8 numerical error\nby 2.6×compared to standard per-tensor quantization.\nThis partly explains why FP8FlashAttention-3 does not perform as well for small sequence length and causal masking\ncompared to the FP8 cuDNN kernels."
     }
    ]
   },
