   {
    "id": "c6ea09bf-d829-45dc-84c9-6df5a6e5491b",
    "title": "MoBA: Mixture of Block Attention for Long-Context LLMs",
    "kind": "literature",
    "path": ".research/sources/c6ea09bf-d829-45dc-84c9-6df5a6e5491b/reference.json",
    "sha256": "1f838093e2e67115f938b48e4d3ccc6cd0a7c2c6e1f2b6850efe0d898bdd1cf8",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:24:09.517Z",
    "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.52202/085713-0602",
    "doi": "10.52202/085713-0602",
    "fullTextPath": ".research/sources/c6ea09bf-d829-45dc-84c9-6df5a6e5491b/fulltext.pdf",
    "coverage": "full-text",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 1
      },
      "text": "We intro-\nduce Mixture of Block Attention (MoBA), an innovative approach that applies the principles of\nMixture of Experts (MoE) to the attention mechanism."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 2
      },
      "text": "Alternatively, a range of dynamic\nsparse attention mechanisms, exemplified by Quest (Tang et al. 2024), Minference\nThus, we introduce Mixture of Block Attention (MoBA), a novel architecture that builds upon the innovative principles\nof Mixture of Experts (MoE) (Shazeer et al. 2017) and applies them to the attention mechanism of the Transformer\nmodel.\nMoBA addresses the computational inefficiency of traditional attention mechanisms by\npartitioning the context into blocks and employing a gating mechanism to selectively route query tokens to the most\nrelevant blocks. This block sparse attention significantly reduces the computational costs, paving the way for more\nefficient processing of long sequences."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 6
      },
      "text": "To assess the effectiveness of MoBA, we perform scaling law experiments by comparing\nthe validation loss of language models trained using either full attention or MoBA."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 7
      },
      "text": "As shown in Figure 3b, although MoBA\nexhibits a marginally higher last block LM loss compared to full attention in all five experiments, the loss gap is\nprogressively narrowing."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 9
      },
      "text": "It\nis particularly noteworthy that in the longest benchmark, RULER, where MoBA operates at a sparsity level of up to\n1 − 4096×12\n128K = 62.5%, Llama-8B-1M-MoBA nearly matches the performance of Llama-8B-1M-Full, with a score of\n0.7818 compared to 0.7849.\nFor context lengths of up to 1M tokens, we evaluate the model using the traditional Needle\nin the Haystack benchmark."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "59da3bba-9e83-4baa-92cd-50ffffb7f333",
    "title": "Native Sparse Attention: Hardware-Aligned and Natively Trainable Sparse Attention",
    "kind": "literature",
    "path": ".research/sources/59da3bba-9e83-4baa-92cd-50ffffb7f333/reference.json",
    "sha256": "46529e5ad7687b360ee3d55328c8931108ff5e1ded1c1c80e0b48436b39d4ac5",
    "revision": 1,
    "importedAt": "2026-09-23T20:24:15.963Z",
    "sourceUrl": "https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.acl-long.1126",
    "doi": "10.18653/v1/2025.acl-long.1126",
    "fullTextPath": ".research/sources/59da3bba-9e83-4baa-92cd-50ffffb7f333/fulltext.pdf",
    "coverage": "full-text",
    "verified": true,
    "stale": false,
    "chunks": [
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 1
      },
      "text": "We present NSA,\na Natively trainable Sparse Attention mecha-\nnism that integrates algorithmic innovations\nwith hardware-aligned optimizations to achieve\nefficient long-context modeling.\nLeft: Despite\nbeing sparse, NSA surpasses Full Attention baseline\non average across general benchmarks, long-context\ntasks, and reasoning evaluation."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 2
      },
      "text": "We evaluate NSA through comprehensive ex-\nperiments on real-world language corpora."
     },
     {
      "locator": {
       "page": 7
      },
      "text": "We also evaluate NSA on LongBench (Bai et al.,\n2023) against state-of-the-art sparse attention meth\nAs shown in Table 2, NSA\nachieves the highest average score 0.469, outper-\nforming all baselines (+0.032 over Full Attention\nand +0.046 over Exact-Top)."
     }
    ]
   },
